  <section id="how-to-read">
    <h2>How to read this page</h2>
    <p>
      The article is a visitor’s guide to a monument in Maykop, the capital of
      the Republic of Adygea in Russia. Amin Samkough is named in a single line
      of it. It is also someone else’s published work, so I have
      <strong>not</strong> translated it in full. This page summarizes it in my
      own words and translates only the short passages about the monument’s
      layout and about Samkough. Please have a Russian reader check the
      translations.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>Highlighted text marked “Review”</mark> is something I’m unsure of.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="overview">
    <h2>What the article is about</h2>
    <p>
      The article describes the monument <span lang="ru">«Единение и согласие»</span>
      (“Unity and Concord”), which locals call <span lang="ru">«Очаг»</span>
      (“Ochag”, meaning “the hearth”). It stands in central Maykop near the
      Cathedral Mosque and the city philharmonic hall. My summary of the
      article:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Getting there.</strong> Directions on foot and by minibus from
        the railway station. The monument can be visited free of charge at any
        hour.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Meaning.</strong> It was opened in summer 2013 as a memorial to
        the victims of the Caucasian War, and as a symbol of unity among the
        republic’s peoples. The hearth stands for the centre of the home, giving
        light and warmth, and the article says the war “put out” thousands of
        hearths across the Caucasus.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Five tiers.</strong> A base that holds the hearth itself; a
        second tier with scenes from Adyghe culture (a New Year dawn greeting,
        a herald calling people home, a Nart hero bringing fire, and a motif
        from a warrior’s mail shirt); a third tier of portraits of notable
        people (see below); a “field of stones” on the fourth tier, as a call to
        gather stones and return home; and the Adyghe symbols of three arrows
        and twelve golden stars on the fifth.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Renovation.</strong> The surroundings were redeveloped in
        2017–2018, with new paths, benches and trees, plus decorative grilles
        and lit stars on the monument.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="samkough">
    <h2>What it says about Amin Samkough</h2>
    <p>
      The third tier is described as showing outstanding members of the Adyghe
      people and famous warriors. The article lists “some of them”, and Samkough
      is one:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p lang="ru">
        Амин Самгуг (Самгугъ Амин) – государственный и политический деятель
        Сирии, просветитель и историк.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>
          “Amin Samgug (Samgug Amin) – state and political figure of Syria,
          educator and historian.”
        </em>
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The article gives no dates for him and no further detail. The others it
      lists on that tier include the Meotian queen Tirgatao, the princes Inal
      and Redede, the scholar Umar Berseyi, the philosopher Kazanoko Zhabagi,
      and several leaders of the 19th-century resistance (Berzeg, Zanoko
      Seferbey and Tuguzhuko Kyzbech).
    </p>

    <h3>Things to review</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>one source for his inclusion.</strong> Among the pages
          I checked, this is the only one that names Samkough. Other pages about
          the monument (a tourism site, an official-style description, a news
          report) confirm that the third tier has bas-reliefs of notable people
          but don’t list who they are. His inclusion should be confirmed from
          photographs of the monument or an official list.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>is this the same Amin Samkough?</strong> Probably, but
          the article doesn’t say so. The description fits him, and its wording
          is close to the headline of the biography on this site (“…State and
          Political Figure and Prominent Educator and Historian”), so it may
          have been taken from the same source or from that biography.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the spelling in parentheses.</strong> “Самгугъ Амин”
          is the Adyghe-language form, with the family name first and the final
          letter «ъ». I’ve transliterated it as “Samgug Amin”.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>opening date.</strong> The article says “summer
          2013”. Another page about the monument gives 19 July 2013.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <hr />

